        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Hardwired, Hydronic or Plug-In: Pick the Type Before the Brand
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people shopping for a baseboard heater are standing in a cold room: a finished basement that the furnace never reaches, an addition with no ductwork, a bedroom over the garage, or a whole house that was built with electric heat in every room. The first decision is not the brand. It is how the heater gets its power, because that decides whether you need an electrician, what it costs to install and how much heat you can put in the room.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hardwired Electric Baseboard (240V and 120V)</h3>

          <p>
            This is the classic long, low metal heater screwed to the wall under a window. A resistance element runs the length of the unit inside aluminum fins, cool air enters at the bottom, warms over the fins and rises out of the top. There is no fan, so it is silent apart from a faint tick as the metal expands. It is wired straight into a circuit from your panel, usually at 240 volts, and it is the cheapest heater per watt you can buy. Cadet, Marley (Qmark, Fahrenheat, Berko) and Stelpro make most of what is sold in North America.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hydronic Electric Baseboard</h3>

          <p>
            A hydronic electric baseboard looks similar but holds a sealed tube of liquid around the element. It is not connected to a boiler or to plumbing; the fluid is factory-filled and stays in the heater. The liquid takes longer to warm up and keeps releasing heat after the thermostat clicks off, which makes the room feel more even and the surface less hot to the touch. It costs several times as much as a convection unit of the same wattage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Portable Plug-In Baseboard-Style Heaters</h3>

          <p>
            These are free-standing heaters on feet, shaped like a baseboard, that plug into a normal 120-volt outlet. Because a standard outlet is limited to about 1,500 watts, they top out there. They need no electrician and you can take them with you when you move, but they are space heaters in a baseboard shape and follow space heater rules: one per outlet, no extension cords, and never left where bedding or curtains can fall on them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Choose in One Line Each</h3>

          <p>
            Own the home and want permanent heat in a room: hardwired 240V. Only a 120V circuit nearby: a hardwired 120V model, sized smaller. Want softer, steadier heat and can pay for it: hydronic. Renting, or need heat this week without opening a wall: plug-in. The picks below include each type so you can match the heater to your situation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Size Baseboard Heater Do I Need? About 10 Watts per Square Foot
          </h2>

          <p>
            Baseboard heaters are sized in watts, and the rule used across the industry is simple: about 10 watts per square foot of floor in a room with standard 8-foot ceilings and ordinary insulation. A 120-square-foot bedroom needs about 1,200 watts; a 200-square-foot living room needs about 2,000 watts. Makers' own room ratings follow the same math. Stelpro, for example, lists its 1,500-watt Brava as heating up to 150 square feet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Go Higher</h3>

          <p>
            Add watts when the room fights you. Rooms with high ceilings, lots of glass, an outside wall on two sides, a slab floor or older insulation often need 12 to 15 watts per square foot, especially in a cold climate. A finished basement with insulated walls can sit near the low end. If the room is already heated by a furnace and you only want to top it up, you can go lower. When in doubt, size up slightly and let the thermostat hold the temperature; an undersized heater runs flat out and still leaves the room cold.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Length Follows Watts</h3>

          <p>
            On a standard convection baseboard, length rises with wattage, roughly 250 watts per foot. A 750-watt unit is about 3 feet long, a 1,000-watt unit about 4 feet and a 1,500-watt unit about 6 feet. Measure the wall under the window before you buy. If one long heater will not fit, two shorter heaters on the same circuit and thermostat give the same total and spread the warmth more evenly.
          </p>

