        <div id="voltage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. 240V vs 120V Baseboard Heaters: Use 240V If You Have It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hardwired baseboard heaters come in two voltages, and the choice is mostly made for you by the wiring in the house. Electrically heated homes almost always run their baseboards at 240 volts; 120-volt models exist for single rooms where only a 120V circuit is practical.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why 240V Is the Default</h3>

          <p>
            Watts are volts times amps. At 240 volts a heater draws half the current of a 120-volt heater of the same wattage, so one circuit can carry twice as much heat. A 240V, 20-amp circuit can run up to about 3,840 watts of baseboard under the usual rule of loading a continuous heating circuit to 80 percent, while a 120V, 20-amp circuit tops out near 1,920 watts. That is why whole-house electric heat is built on 240V.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When 120V Makes Sense</h3>

          <p>
            A 120V heater is reasonable for a small bathroom, a closet office or a single small room where running a new 240V line would mean opening finished walls. Keep it small: 500 to 1,000 watts is typical. It still wants its own circuit, or at least a circuit with nothing else meaningful on it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Swap Voltages</h3>

          <p>
            A 240V heater on 120V puts out only a quarter of its rated heat. A 120V heater on 240V will overheat and can fail dangerously. Many 240V units also list a lower output at 208V, the voltage found in some apartment buildings: the Cadet 3F750W listing, for example, gives 750 watts at 240V and 563 watts at 208V. Read the label, match the voltage, and if you live in an apartment, ask which you have before buying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Installing a Hardwired Baseboard Heater: Dedicated Circuit, Often a Permit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mounting the heater is the easy part: a few screws into studs and a junction box at one end. The work that matters happens at the panel, and it is where most of the cost and most of the risk sits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Needs a Dedicated Circuit</h3>

          <p>
            Electric heat is treated as a continuous load, so baseboard heaters go on their own circuit sized for them, not tapped into a bedroom receptacle circuit. One circuit can feed several heaters in the same or neighbouring rooms as long as the total stays within its limit. A 240V circuit uses a double-pole breaker; a 120V circuit uses a single-pole breaker.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Breaker, Wire and Panel Space</h3>

          <p>
            Typical residential runs are 15 or 20 amps with wire gauge to match: 14-gauge copper for 15 amps, 12-gauge for 20 amps. You also need room in the panel for the breaker and enough service capacity for the new load. Older homes with full panels sometimes need a subpanel or a service upgrade before any heater goes in, which can cost more than the heaters themselves.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Permits and Electricians</h3>

          <p>
            Most jurisdictions require a permit for a new circuit, and many require a licensed electrician to pull it or at least an inspection if you do it yourself. Replacing an existing heater with the same voltage and wattage on the same circuit is a much smaller job that many handy owners do themselves with the breaker off and the circuit tested dead. Anything new, check your local rules first. Insurance claims after a fire can turn on whether the work was permitted.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Plug-In Avoids</h3>

          <p>
            A plug-in baseboard heater skips all of this. It is the right answer for renters and for anyone who needs heat now. The trade is lower output, one heater per outlet, and a cord that has to stay clear of traffic and furniture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="thermostats" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Line-Voltage vs Smart Thermostats: The Thermostat Is Half the Heater
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most hardwired baseboard heaters ship without a thermostat. You choose between a built-in kit that mounts on the end of the heater and a wall thermostat, and that choice affects comfort as much as the heater does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Built-In vs Wall-Mounted</h3>

          <p>
            A built-in thermostat, such as Cadet's BTF2W kit for its F Series, is cheap and easy because it sits in the heater's own wiring compartment. Its weakness is that it reads the air right at the heater, near the floor and often near a cold window, so it cycles on readings that are not what you feel at the couch. A wall thermostat, mounted about 5 feet up on an inside wall, reads the room properly and can control several heaters at once.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Line-Voltage: Single Pole vs Double Pole</h3>

          <p>
            Baseboard thermostats are line-voltage: the full 120 or 240 volts passes through them. A single-pole thermostat breaks one leg and suits 120V heaters. On 240V a single-pole unit leaves one leg energized even when set to the lowest temperature. A double-pole thermostat, like Cadet's double-pole line-voltage model or Fahrenheat's FTA2A, breaks both legs and has a true off position. For 240V heaters, double-pole is the safer choice. Check the thermostat's amp rating, commonly 22 amps, against the total load of the heaters it controls.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Smart Line-Voltage Thermostats</h3>

          <p>
            Smart thermostats made for baseboards, such as the Mysa V2 and Mysa LITE, the meross models built for electric baseboards and Honeywell Home's programmable RLV4305A1000, replace the old dial and add schedules and phone control. Mysa markets savings of up to 26 percent, which comes from turning heat down when you are out or asleep, not from the heater getting more efficient. Check the wiring needs before you buy: meross states four wires are needed for one model, and some smart units have a lower amp rating than the dial they replace.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Your Nest or ecobee Will Not Work Directly</h3>

          <p>
            Thermostats built for furnaces and heat pumps run on 24 volts. Wired to a baseboard circuit they will be destroyed, and they can start a fire. They can only control baseboard heat through a separate relay, which adds cost and complexity. Our <a href="/best-smart-thermostat/">best smart thermostat guide</a> covers both kinds, including the Mysa for baseboard heat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hydronic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Hydronic Baseboard Heat More Efficient? No, but It Feels Better
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sales copy for hydronic electric baseboards often implies they cost less to run. The honest answer is that every electric resistance heater converts close to 100 percent of the electricity it uses into heat. A 750-watt hydronic heater and a 750-watt convection heater put the same amount of heat into the room for each kilowatt-hour you pay for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Hydronic Really Changes</h3>

          <p>
            The fluid stores heat. A convection baseboard warms up and cools down quickly, so the room swings a degree or two as the thermostat clicks on and off. A hydronic unit heats up slowly and keeps giving off warmth after it switches off, which smooths those swings. The surface also tends to run cooler, and the heater is quieter because there is less rapid expansion of metal. Many people find the room more comfortable, and some set the thermostat a degree lower as a result. That is where any real saving comes from.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When It Is Worth Paying For</h3>

          <p>
            Hydronic is worth it in bedrooms where the tick of a convection unit bothers you, in rooms where you sit still for hours, and for anyone sensitive to the dry, hot-surface feel of a standard baseboard. It is not worth it if your goal is a lower bill. For that, look at insulation, a better thermostat, or a heat pump.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hydronic Is Not the Same as a Boiler System</h3>

          <p>
            Hot-water baseboard fed by a gas or oil boiler is also called hydronic. That is a different system, plumbed to a boiler, and the heaters in this guide do not connect to it. Electric hydronic units are self-contained and wire up like any other electric baseboard.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Baseboard Heater Safety: Clearances, Outlets and UL/ETL Listing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Baseboard heaters are among the safer ways to heat a room because they have no flame and no fuel, but their surface gets hot and they sit at floor level, right where curtains, bedding, toys and furniture end up. Most baseboard fires start with something touching or covering the heater.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Curtains and Furniture</h3>

          <p>
            Follow the clearances in your heater's manual, which commonly ask for several inches above the heater for draperies and about a foot in front for furniture. Curtains should hang clear of the heater, not drape over it. Do not push a bed, sofa or bookcase against it, and do not let kids store toys or clothes along it. A blocked heater also heats poorly, because air cannot flow through the fins.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Mount One Under an Outlet</h3>

          <p>
            Electrical codes and makers' instructions prohibit installing a baseboard heater below a wall receptacle in most cases, because a lamp or phone cord hanging down would rest on the hot surface. Plan the heater location around the outlets, not the other way round.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Look for UL or ETL</h3>

          <p>
            Buy a heater that is listed by a recognised testing lab, usually shown as UL or ETL on the label. Inspectors check for it on hardwired heat, and insurers care about it after a fire. Established brands list their heaters as a matter of course; cheap unbranded units sold online are where you are most likely to find none.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Extra Rules for Plug-In Models</h3>

          <p>
            Plug a portable baseboard heater straight into a wall outlet, never into an extension cord or power strip, and keep it on a hard floor away from beds and drapes. Look for overheat protection and, on free-standing models, a tip-over switch. Our <a href="/best-electric-space-heater/">electric space heater guide</a> covers the full set of portable heater safety rules.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Much Does a Baseboard Heater Cost to Run? Watts Times Hours
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running cost is plain arithmetic. A heater's wattage divided by 1,000 is the kilowatt-hours it uses per hour of full output. Multiply by your electricity rate from your bill and by the hours it actually runs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Math</h3>

          <p>
            A 1,000-watt heater running flat out uses 1 kilowatt-hour per hour. At an example rate of 17 cents per kilowatt-hour, that is 17 cents an hour. A heater rarely runs nonstop: once the room reaches temperature, the thermostat cycles it on and off, so in a reasonably insulated room it might run a third to half of the time in cold weather. Your bill depends far more on the room, the climate and your thermostat setting than on the brand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Zoning Is Where You Save</h3>

          <p>
            The genuine advantage of baseboard heat is that each room has its own control. Turn the spare room and the guest bath down to 55 degrees, keep the bedroom cool during the day and the living room cool at night, and you only pay to heat the rooms in use. A programmable or smart thermostat makes that automatic.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Heat Pump Beats Baseboards</h3>

          <p>
            If you are heating a whole house or a large addition with electricity, a ductless mini-split heat pump typically delivers two to three units of heat for each unit of electricity, against one for any baseboard. It costs far more upfront, but in many climates it pays back over several winters and adds cooling. See our <a href="/best-mini-split-heat-pump/">best mini-split heat pump guide</a>. Baseboards remain the right call for small rooms, low-use rooms and backup heat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-space-heater" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Baseboard Heater vs Space Heater vs Oil-Filled Radiator
          </h2>

          <p>
            A plug-in baseboard heater, a tower space heater and an oil-filled radiator all draw the same 1,500 watts from a wall outlet and produce the same amount of heat. They differ in how they deliver it, how loud they are and where they fit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Baseboard Heater</h3>

          <p>
            Long and low, it sits along a wall and sets up a slow convection loop across the room. It is silent and works best under a window, where it counters the cold draft coming off the glass. A hardwired one is the only option here that becomes a permanent part of the house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fan Space Heater</h3>

          <p>
            A ceramic or tower heater uses a fan to push warm air where you point it, so you feel heat sooner. It is louder and better for warming a person at a desk than an entire room. Our <a href="/best-electric-space-heater/">electric space heater guide</a> ranks the best of these.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oil-Filled Radiator</h3>

          <p>
            An oil-filled radiator behaves like a portable hydronic baseboard: slow to warm, silent, and steady once it is going. It takes up less wall length but more floor depth. If you like the idea of hydronic heat but do not want a 5-foot unit, see our <a href="/best-oil-filled-radiator-heater/">oil-filled radiator heater guide</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which One for Your Room</h3>

          <p>
            Permanent heat in a room you own: hardwired baseboard. Silent steady heat you can move: oil-filled radiator or portable hydronic baseboard. Quick heat at a desk: fan heater. Most homes with a single cold room are best served by one hardwired baseboard on a wall thermostat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Hydronic to Lower the Bill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hydronic baseboards feel steadier and quieter, but every electric resistance heater turns a watt into the same heat. If your goal is a smaller bill, spend the money on insulation, a programmable thermostat or a mini-split heat pump instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing by Room Name Instead of Square Feet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bedroom can be 90 or 250 square feet. Measure the floor, multiply by about 10 watts per square foot, and add more for big windows, high ceilings or poor insulation. An undersized heater runs constantly and still leaves the room cold.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing Up 120V and 240V Models</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 240V heater on 120V gives a quarter of its heat; a 120V heater on 240V overheats. Model numbers often differ by one suffix, so check the label voltage against your circuit before you install anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Single-Pole Thermostat on a 240V Heater</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single-pole thermostat leaves one leg of a 240V heater live even at its lowest setting. Use a double-pole line-voltage thermostat with a true off position, rated for the total amps of the heaters it controls.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pushing Furniture and Curtains Against It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sofa or bed against a baseboard, or curtains draping over it, is how most baseboard fires start, and it also blocks the airflow the heater needs. Keep the clearances in the manual and never mount one under an outlet.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

