        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best baseboard heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most rooms, the best baseboard heater is a hardwired 240V convection unit sized at about 10 watts per square foot, such as the Cadet F Series 4F1000W, controlled by a double-pole wall thermostat. Pick the Stelpro Brava for larger or quieter rooms, the Cadet SoftHeat for steady hydronic heat, and the Cadet portable if you cannot hardwire.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are baseboard heaters expensive to run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They cost the same per watt as any electric resistance heater. A 1,000-watt heater running at full output uses 1 kilowatt-hour per hour, so at 17 cents per kilowatt-hour that is 17 cents an hour. Thermostat cycling cuts real running time, and heating only the rooms you use is where baseboards save money. A heat pump is cheaper per unit of heat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is hydronic baseboard heat cheaper than regular electric baseboard?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Both convert electricity to heat at nearly 100 percent, so a 750-watt hydronic unit costs the same to run as a 750-watt convection unit. Hydronic heaters hold heat in sealed fluid, so they cycle more gently, run quieter and feel more even. Some people set the thermostat lower because of that comfort, which is the only real saving.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many watts of baseboard heat do I need per square foot?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan on about 10 watts per square foot for a room with 8-foot ceilings and normal insulation. A 150-square-foot room needs about 1,500 watts. Go up to 12 to 15 watts per square foot for high ceilings, big windows, slab floors, poor insulation or a very cold climate, and lower if the furnace already partly heats the room.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a baseboard heater myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mounting the heater is easy, but a new hardwired heater needs a dedicated circuit with the right breaker and wire, and most areas require a permit and often a licensed electrician. Swapping a heater for one of the same voltage and wattage is simpler. If you are not sure, hire an electrician, or use a plug-in heater that needs no wiring.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a smart thermostat with baseboard heaters?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but only one built for line-voltage electric heat, such as the Mysa, the meross baseboard models or Honeywell Home's RLV4305A1000. Standard 24-volt thermostats like the Nest or ecobee cannot be wired to a baseboard circuit without a relay, and connecting one directly can destroy it and create a fire risk. Check the thermostat's amp rating and wiring needs first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How far should furniture and curtains be from a baseboard heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow your heater's manual, which commonly asks for several inches between the top of the heater and draperies and about a foot of clear space in front for furniture. Curtains should hang clear rather than drape over the unit. Never mount a baseboard heater beneath a wall outlet, and keep beds, sofas, toys and clothing away from it.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

